::meta{crate: tomet-validator-core, dependencies: list(tomet-address, tomet-ast, tomet-cst, tomet-declaration, tomet-lexer, tomet-semantics, tomet-tree, tomet-type), dependents: list(tomet-validator, tomet-validator-blueprint, tomet-validator-rule)}

=[tomet-validator-core]

Core document structure and semantic validation.

Validates standard and custom elements, duplicate IDs, singleton directives,
regions (preamble vs body), argument declarations, and dangling intra-document anchors.

==[Module diagnostic]

===[Enums]

====[enum Severity]

How much a [`Diagnostic`] matters.

Derived from the variant rather than stored: severity is a property of
the rule, not of the occurrence, so keeping a field would be a second
place the same fact lives.

====[enum Diagnostic]

Something `validate_document` found and a reader should know about.

Named for what the callers already called it: `bindings/{js,java,
python}` each bind the result to `diagnostics`. It was
`ValidationError` while every variant really was an error; `Draft` and
`Fixme` are not, so the name moved rather than the meaning stretching.

====[enum CstValidationError]

A validation error tied directly to precise byte ranges in the CST.

==[Module id]

===[Functions]

====[fn collect_ids]

Walks every node in `doc` and returns `(id, span)` for each
[`Element::id`]/[`Section::id`] found, in document order.

====[fn collect_ids_cst]

Scans `root` for every `#(id)` slot and returns the exact [`TextRange`]
of each id's value, in document order.

Mirrors [`collect_ids`]. No owner-kind allowlist or `@link` exclusion
is needed the way the old `MAP_ENTRY`-scanning version needed both:
an `ID_GROUP` node can only ever appear where `parse_groups`/
`parse_connect` put one, so there is nothing else it could be mistaken
for, and no attribute-key collision with `@link`'s own `id` argument
to guard against (that argument is not this slot).

==[Structs]

===[struct Declared]

A declaration paired with its source span.

==[Functions]

===[fn classify_in]

Classifies `el` against `std` and the namespaces in scope.

===[fn shape_str]

===[fn validate_core]

Runs core element, ID, region, and argument validations on `doc`.

===[fn check_singletons_and_regions]

Enforces the `singleton` and `region` axes.

===[fn check_unfinished]

===[fn check_arguments]

===[fn check_document_declarations]

Runs checks on the standard declarations in `doc`, mapping each [`Problem`]
back to a [`Diagnostic`] with the declaration element's span.

===[fn check_ctmt_declarations]

Validates declarations from a `.ctmt` file header against [`Host::Ctmt`].

Reports host restrictions, duplicate singletons, version format errors,
too many arguments, and invalid macro/var definitions. Declarations whose
names are not standard are ignored (their meaning belongs to the tool that
reads the file).

===[fn check_content_shape]

===[fn check_vocabulary_params]

Checks how a vocabulary document declares its parameters: a `type:` that names
no type, a `default:` that is not of its `type:`. The same findings are reported
where a vault reads the vocabulary ([`tomet_semantics::LoadedVocabularies`]);
this is for checking the vocabulary file itself.

===[fn check_retired_settings_keys]

===[fn check_field_types]

Checks the form of the `::meta` fields the settings declare a `type:` for.

`field_types` is the settings' `meta` declarations as `name -> type`,
which is all this needs of them: the validator sits below the crate that
reads settings, so the caller hands the declarations over as plain data.

Only a `type:` with a form can be wrong: `uuid` and `datetime`. A field the
document does not write, an empty one (the state in which `tomet format`
generates one), and a `${...}` not yet evaluated are not reported.

===[fn check_dangling_anchors]

===[fn validate_cst]
